#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204654} :activity/id #uuid "bc91f477-9286-5008-a9ee-eb408291421a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050176 :topic/id #uuid "340a9570-0274-5535-83f9-7912961ba2ff" :topic/math-academy-id 4826} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204654 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205236 :tutorial/content "Recall that a binary relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product\n\n$$\nA\\times A = A^2{:}\n$$\n\n$$\nR\\subseteq A^2\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we have the following notations:\n\n- $x \\: R \\: y$ if and only if $(x,y) \\in R$\n- $\\require{cancel}x \\: \\cancel{\\!R\\!} \\: y$ if and only if $(x,y) \\notin R$\n\nRelations can be defined over infinite sets. For example, consider the relation $R$ on $\\mathbb{Z}$ defined by\n\n$$\nx \\: R \\: y \\qquad \\Leftrightarrow \\qquad x \\geq y\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $x \\: R \\: y$ if and only if\n\n$$\nx \\geq y\n$$\n\nFor this relation, note that\n\n- the symbols $R$ and $\\geq$ are interchangeable, and\n- the symbols $\\cancel{\\!R\\!}$ and $\\not\\geq$ (or, equivalently, $\\cancel{\\!R\\!}$ and $\\lt$) are also interchangeable.\n\nFor example:\n\n- $2 \\: R \\: 0$ since $2 \\geq 0$\n- $\\require{cancel} 1 \\: \\cancel{\\!R\\!} \\: \\pi$ since $1 \\not\\geq \\pi$\n- $0 \\: R \\: 0$ since $0 \\geq 0$\n\nFinally, since $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y) \\in \\mathbb{Z}^2$ such that\n\n$$\nx \\geq y\n$$\n\nwe can write out $R$ in set-builder notation as\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{(x,y) \\in \\mathbb{Z}^2 \\:: \\: x \\geq y \\big\\}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f63d42cd-e795-5986-8ce3-11dc8e48fcaa" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9060 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "8b79cebe-4264-5034-b9e7-c2090474f283" :step/math-academy-id 152220 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204655}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204655 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203899 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203695 :question/id #uuid "1488c9f